 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "LIBRARY OF BIRMINGHAM. Opened in 2013, wrapped in metal rings like a giant piece of jewellery, which suits the city. It has a rooftop garden, a Shakespeare Memorial Room and more books than you could read."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "CENTENARY SQUARE. The city's big public living room, with fountains, statues, and a very fine view of the library. Brummies will argue about what the statues are called. They will also offer you tea during the argument."
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "THE BULL. A giant bronze bull has stood outside the Bullring since 2003. The Bullring has been a market since 1166, so the bull is, if anything, a bit late. Please do not ride it. People have tried."
  },
  {
   "x": 57,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "ST MARTIN IN THE BULLRING. A church has stood here for around 800 years, and the market has been bustling round it ever since. Choirs, bells, and a very patient vicar."
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "SELFRIDGES. A building covered in 15,000 shiny aluminium discs, which look like a very expensive sequinned armadillo. It is the most photographed building in the city, and it has never once complained."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "THE ROTUNDA. A round 1960s office tower, saved from demolition and turned into flats. Birmingham has more canal mileage than Venice, and a surprising number of round buildings."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "BIRMINGHAM'S CANALS. The city has about 35 miles of canal, which is more than Venice. Narrowboats still drift through the heart of town. They have right of way. They also have tea."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "THE CUSTARD FACTORY. Once the Bird's Custard works. Alfred Bird invented egg-free custard here in 1837 so his wife, who couldn't eat eggs, could join in the pudding. A kind idea, in a bowl."
  },
  {
   "x": 51,
   "y": 44,
   "text": "BACK-TO-BACK HOUSES. Birmingham once had thousands of homes built in rows, sharing walls on three sides. A few survive as a museum. They are small, snug, and rather harder to get a sofa into than they look."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "THE JEWELLERY QUARTER. About 40% of all the jewellery made in Britain still comes from this one square mile. The clock tower is a memorial and a great place to say 'meet you under the clock'."
  }
 ],
